## Report
Validated the finding. I built and exercised the checked-out commit 93c65d758807d51e1149d69d507402c090299157. Code review confirmed that the production enablement guard only checks frontend role and that ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR parses to a loopback SocketAddr: crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:308-324. argmin-s3 then passes config.local_debug_endpoint directly into server-http: crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:393-398. In request handling, the local debug check occurs at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:663-672 and returns immediately with no request permit; the normal request-admission and S3 parsing path starts afterward at serve.rs:676 onward, and normal S3 authentication is later in HttpFrontend::handle_s3_request at crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:872-880. The debug endpoint implementation returns metrics for GET /__argmin/debug/metrics and triggers observability::dump_flight_recorder_to_stderr for POST /__argmin/debug/flight-recorder/dump at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:909-935; metric names are formatted at serve.rs:957-983. Dynamic reproduction against the actual argmin-s3 binary: with ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=true and ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:19080, an HTTP request with no Authorization header returned HTTP/1.1 200 OK and metrics including inflight_requests, request_error_total, storage_rpc_error_total, metadata_command_conflict_total. An unauthenticated POST to /__argmin/debug/flight-recorder/dump returned HTTP/1.1 204 No Content and server stderr contained == argmin flight recorder dump reason="local-debug-endpoint" records=1 ==. For contrast, an unauthenticated normal S3 GET / returned HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden AccessDenied. Startup guard behavior was also confirmed: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=true with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR=0.0.0.0:19081 exited with rc=1 and configuration error: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT requires ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR to be loopback. Targeted tests also passed: cargo test -p server-http local_debug -- --nocapture showed 3 passed, including local_debug_metrics_endpoint_bypasses_request_admission and the POST-only dump test; cargo test -p argmin-s3 local_debug_endpoint -- --nocapture showed 4 passed for default-disabled, loopback-accepted, non-loopback-rejected, and storage-node-only-rejected cases. I attempted crash/memory tooling as required: the debug build and PoC did not crash, which is expected for this authorization/diagnostics exposure; valgrind was unavailable in the container (rc=127, command not found); Rust ASan required nightly (-Z sanitizer=address rejected by stable rustc). I used lldb non-interactively and set a breakpoint on local_debug_response; the debugger stopped at crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:910 with backtrace frame #1 at handle serve.rs:664, proving the debug request enters the bypass branch before the later normal path. This validates the suspected issue: when the feature is enabled, any client that can reach the loopback listener, including an untrusted local process or traffic forwarded by a reverse proxy, can access diagnostics without S3 credentials or separate admin authentication. Impact matches the finding: the HTTP response leaks internal counters and POST causes bounded stderr flight-recorder dumping, but it does not directly return object contents, credentials, headers, or recorder contents.

